  "verbalization": "Oblonganoside C is shown to have targets that ultimately lead to the Muscle nicotinic acetylcholine receptor, through a series of molecular interactions. The first pathway links Oblonganoside C to Serine/threonine-protein kinase ICK, which is targeted by Ent-Gloeosteretriol, and Ent-Gloeosteretriol is known to interact with the Muscle nicotinic acetylcholine receptor. The second pathway connects Oblonganoside C to Tyrosine-protein kinase Fes/Fps, which is targeted by a complex compound, and this compound also targets the Muscle nicotinic acetylcholine receptor. Both pathways suggest that Oblonganoside C may influence the function of the nicotinic acetylcholine receptor through different kinases, potentially modulating receptor activity or signaling. The evidence supports a possible role for Oblonganoside C in regulating nicotinic acetylcholine receptor function, though the exact mechanism remains to be elucidated. The strength of the evidence is moderate, with some pathways showing higher confidence scores, but the overall relationship is supported by multiple independent targets leading to the same receptor.",
